    "message": {
     "content": "This is an **exceptionally strong offer**. It sits at the top end (90th+ percentile) of Google\u2019s L6 Product Management compensation band and represents a significant financial upgrade over your current Meta package.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Breakdown of the Numbers\n\nTo evaluate this accurately, it is essential to understand **Google\u2019s front-loaded vesting schedule**. \n\nUnlike Meta (which typically vests RSUs evenly at 25% per year), Google\u2019s standard 4-year new-hire equity vests **33% in Year 1, 33% in Year 2, 22% in Year 3, and 12% in Year 4**.\n\n| Component | Meta (IC6 PM) Current | Google (L6 PM) Year 1 | Google (L6 PM) Year 2 | Google (L6 PM) 4-Yr Annual Avg |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $260,000 | $260,000 | $260,000 |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $52,000 (20%) | $52,000 (20%) | $52,000 |\n| **RSUs (Stock)** | $200,000\u2013$250,000 | $442,200 *(33%)* | $442,200 *(33%)* | $335,000 *(25% avg)* |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | \u2014 | $200,000 *(one-time)* | \u2014 | $50,000 *(amortized)* |\n| **Total Compensation** | **~$476,000\u2013$526,000** | **~$954,200** | **~$754,200** | **~$697,000/yr** |\n\n#### Key Takeaways:\n- **Year 1:** You will make **~$954k**, an increase of over **80%** compared to your current Meta compensation.\n- **Year 2:** You will make **~$754k** (excluding any new refresher grants awarded in Year 1).\n- **The \"Year 3 & 4 Cliff\":** In Year 3, your initial grant drops to 22% ($294.8k), and in Year 4 to 12% ($160.8k). Google designs it this way expecting annual equity refreshers (which vest evenly at 25%/year) to stack on top and fill that gap.\n- **Market Benchmarks:** A typical Google L6 PM total compensation averages **$500,000\u2013$600,000/yr**. At **$1.34M in RSUs + $200k sign-on**, your offer is already at the top of the band (often reserved for high-priority hires or strong counter-offers).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Should You Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n* **Do not walk away:** The compensation and scope make this an incredible career and wealth-building opportunity.\n* **You should negotiate, but strategically:** Because you are happy at Meta and were headhunted, you have maximum leverage. However, because this offer is already near the upper band limits, you should negotiate politely and constructively without making rigid ultimatums.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Negotiate and How Much to Ask For\n\nWhen negotiating an offer that is already high-tier, target specific components where recruiters have discretion:\n\n#### 1. Base Salary ($260k $\\rightarrow$ $270k)\n- **The context:** The posted range caps at $271,000. You are at $260,000.\n- **The ask:** Ask to bump the base to **$270,000** (or the top of the band at $271,000). Base salary is compounding because bonuses and raises are pegged to it.\n\n#### 2. Equity ($1.34M $\\rightarrow$ $1.5M)\n- **The context:** Equity is the easiest lever for big tech compensation committees.\n- **The ask:** Counter at **$1.5M over 4 years** (approx. $375k/yr). This gives you an additional buffer against the Year 3/4 drop-off.\n\n#### 3. Sign-On Terms\n- **The context:** $200k is already high for L6 (standard is $50k\u2013$100k). \n- **The check:** Confirm the clawback and payout terms (e.g., paid fully in month 1 with a 12-month prorated clawback, or split over 2 years).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Negotiation Script\n\n> *\"Thank you for walking me through the offer. I've really enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m genuinely excited about the scope of the role and the impact I could have at Google.*\n>\n> *As you know, I\u2019m in a strong position at Meta and wasn't actively looking to move. To make this an unequivocal 'yes' and offset the unvested equity and trajectory I\u2019d be walking away from, I would be ready to sign if we can get to:*\n>\n> * * **Base Salary:** $270,000 (near the top of the advertised range)*\n> * * **Initial Equity:** $1,500,000 over 4 years*\n>\n> *If we can close the gap on those two numbers, I\u2019m ready to sign immediately.\"*\n\n### Final Verdict\nIf Google agrees to even a partial increase, sign it. If they hold firm and say this is their \"best and final,\" **you should still accept it**\u2014the Year 1\u20132 upside ($1.7M+ total across two years) is difficult to beat at the L6/IC6 level anywhere in the Bay Area.",
